ars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nThen format:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            record = _ShallowRecord? \n```\n\nProblem: we must not alter the original record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. Formatter uses `record.message` for multi-line alignment. Simplest: compute the truncated message and build output manually? But we want to reuse super().format behavior (exception handling etc.) without mutating the original.\n\nApproach: create a copy of the record using `copy.copy(record)` (shallow copy) and set `r.message = truncated` and `r.msg = truncated`, `r.args = None`? Actually if we set msg to truncated and args to (), getMessage returns msg. Since it's a shallow copy, exc_info/stack_info shared but not rewritten on original. The task says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — copying record and modifying copy is fine. But shallow copy sets attributes on the copy only; exc_info not modified. But `_logRecord`... need `copy` import. But careful: `logging.Formatter.format` uses `record.getMessage()`, and sets `record.message = record.getMessage()` (mutating the copy, fine). Also uses `record.exc_text` caching — if record already has exc_text computed by another handler, it reuses. That's fine as it's the same exception info.\n\nBut we want to replace after substitution but exclude exc_text/stack_info from truncation. Using a copy: set copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = () so getMessage returns truncated exactly, and format adds exception text separately. Good.\n\nAlso `forma