ssage is the truncated message. In the original code, `msg.find(record.message)` — if record.message isn't in msg... e.g. truncated message is in msg (msg ends with message). Works.\n\nHowever there's a subtlety: if exception text present, record.message prefix still at position after prefix. Fine.\n\nBut careful with color: The color wrapping is applied after.\n\nAlso important: if the copy is not used and record is the copied one, we should still use the original record for `record.levelno` etc. That's same values.\n\nAlternative approach avoiding copy: temporarily compute using record.getMessage and build via self._style? Simpler approach:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        return self._format_record(record)\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return self._format_record(record)\n    copy_record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n    copy_record.msg = _truncate(message, max_chars)\n    copy_record.args = None\n    return self._format_record(copy_record)\n```\n\nBut wait: if record.message was already set, copy_record.message = full old message, but getMessage() returns msg % args only if args else str(msg). Actually getMessage:\n\n```\nmsg = str(self.msg)\nif self.args:\n    msg = msg % self.args\nreturn msg\n```\nSo copy_record.message gets overwritten in format by `record.message = record.getMessage()`. Good.\n\nHmm, but the copy has `exc_text` maybe cached from previous handler — Formatter.format checks `if record.exc_info: if not record.exc_text: record.exc_text = ...`. If a previous handler already formatted with exc_text computed, copy uses the cached text — that's fine and includes full traceback.\n\nNow `_truncate`: marker = \"...<omitted>...\", len 15. remaining = max_chars - 15. head = (remaining + 1) // 2, tail = remaining - head. Return message[:head] + marker + message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else message[:head]+marker.\n\nCheck max_chars=32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓ matches example.\n\nNow default init:\n\n```\nfmt = NewLineFormatter(_FORMAT, datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT, use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(), max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)\n```\n\nNeed import from deepinfer.environ import envs — check for circular imports: environ.py imports only stdlib. logger imports environ — fine.\n\nNote: validation in formatter for max_message_chars — config already validated >=32.\n\nCareful about `_setup_logger` being called at import: reading env var at import time caches it. Test flow: read 32, set env 64, reinit logger → uses cached 32; then rearm → 64. Since setup runs at import, first access reads env at import. Hmm — but tests may set env var before importing? The task says the test sets it. There's a risk: import of module logger triggers read and caches. Then test sets env var... but they said \"先读取 32，再把环境变量改成 64，重新初始化日志仍使用缓存的32\" implying first access happens explicitly. If the test sets env before importing logger module, fine. Our design follows requirement: read config at init.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: If module is imported before env set, then a test that sets env then calls _setup_logger expects 64? The requirement says cached value; that's their semantics. Follow spec.\n\nAlso \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code handles.\n\nNow stats_logger: change `logger.info(m)`. But note stats_logger's logger is `init_logger(__name__)` = \"deepinfer.engine.stats_logger\" → propagates to root with default handler. Add extra:\n\n```\nlogger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})\n```\nBut nested records... fine. Careful: extra keys can't clash with LogRecord attributes; keep_full_message fine.\n\nAlso note the log worker's `time.sleep` uses dist — not our concern.\n\nLet me check stats_logger lines around 116 for exact text and whether there are other logger.info consuming queue. Let's read the file region. Also need to confirm environ.py around logging section for insertion point, plus rearm functions (already have). And logger.py lines 66-110 (have).\n\nI need to check whether `logger.info(m)` appears multiple times. Let me search.\n\nAlso check imports in logger.py: datetime, logging, multiprocessing, os, sys, threading, time. environ import needed.\n\nLet's do targeted search and read."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 9793, "completion_tokens": 1912, "total_tokens": 11705, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4096}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 1791}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4096, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 5697}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
